    level: "C1",
    text: "Telling time in English involves more than just reading numbers on a clock. The 12-hour clock is standard in daily conversation, using 'a.m.' (ante meridiem — before noon) and 'p.m.' (post meridiem — after noon). When the minute hand points to 12, it's 'o'clock' — three o'clock, seven o'clock. 'Quarter past' means 15 minutes after the hour, 'half past' means 30 minutes after, and 'quarter to' means 15 minutes before the next hour. For other times, English uses 'past' for the first half of the hour (ten past three = 3:10) and 'to' for the second half (twenty to four = 3:40). The 24-hour clock is used in timetables, military contexts, and formal schedules — fourteen hundred hours (14:00) rather than two p.m. Noon is 12:00 p.m. and midnight is 12:00 a.m., though saying 'noon' and 'midnight' avoids confusion. Time-related vocabulary extends to periods of the day: dawn (sunrise), morning, midday, afternoon, dusk (sunset), evening, and night. Approximate times use phrases like 'around', 'about', 'just after', 'just before', 'nearly', and 'almost'. Understanding how English speakers discuss time — punctuality, scheduling, and time zones — is essential for professional and social communication.",
